    <h1>About Tingaroo</h1>

    <p>Tingaroo is a free, open Scratch and STEM curriculum for grades 3 to 8. It is written for the adults who run it: parents, homeschoolers and teachers.</p>
    <!-- CONFIRM: no lessons are published yet. Is "The first lessons are in progress." the right way to say this? -->
    <p>The first lessons are in progress.</p>

    <h2>Who makes Tingaroo</h2>
    <p>Tingaroo is made by Basheer, a maker-educator in Kozhikode, Kerala, India. He runs a hands-on STEM space for K-12 students, where kids build with code and electronics.</p>
    <!-- CONFIRM: should the STEM space be named here? Add its name and link if so. -->
    <p>See more of his work at <a href="https://www.basheer.diy">basheer.diy</a>.</p>

    <h2>License</h2>
    <!-- CONFIRM: the license is described as intended. Change to "are shared under" once lessons are published with the license notice. -->
    <p>Tingaroo lessons are intended to be shared under the <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International license (CC BY-SA 4.0)</a>.</p>
    <p>Under that license, you can copy, share and adapt the lessons, including for paid classes, as long as you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Credit Tingaroo and link to the license.</li>
      <li>Say whether you made changes.</li>
      <li>Share your adapted version under the same license.</li>
    </ul>
    <!-- CONFIRM: check this scope sentence before launch. -->
    <p>The license covers Tingaroo's own text, images and project files. It does not cover Scratch, TurboWarp or their names and logos.</p>

    <h2>Independence</h2>
    <p>Tingaroo is independent. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Scratch Team, the MIT Media Lab or TurboWarp.</p>
